o \"aceg\"). After that the chosen string t is appended to the end of s.Mister B soon found the game boring and came up with the following question: what can be the minimum possible number of different letters in string s on the segment between positions l and r, inclusive. Letters of string s are numerated starting from 1.\n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the Java 8 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\nimport java.io.*;\nimport java.util.*;\n\npublic class Div2_421C {\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {\n        BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));\n        String[] input = reader.readLine().split(\" \");\n        int a = Integer.parseInt(input[0]);\n        int b = Integer.parseInt(input[1]);\n        int l = Integer.parseInt(input[2]);\n        int r = Integer.parseInt(input[3]);\n\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        for (int i = 0; i < a; i++) {\n            sb.append((char) ('a' + i));\n        }\n        char last = sb.charAt(sb.length() - 1);\n        sb.append(new String(new char[b]).replace(\"\\0\", String.valueOf(last)));\n\n        Set<Integer> used = new TreeSet<>();\n        int start = sb.length() - a;\n        for (int i = start; i < sb.length(); i++) {\n            used.add(sb.charAt(i) - 'a');\n        }\n\n        int next = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i < a; i++) {\n            while (used.contains(next)) next++;\n            sb.append((char) ('a' + next));\n            next++;\n        }\n\n        last = sb.charAt(sb.length() - 1);\n        sb.append(new String(new char[b]).replace(\"\\0\", String.valueOf(last)));\n\n        int len = sb.length();\n        used.clear();\n\n        if (r - l + 1 >= len) {\n            for (int i = 0; i < len; i++) {\n                used.add(sb.charAt(i) - 'a');\n            }\n        } else {\n            for (int i = l - 1; i < r; i++) {\n                used.add(sb.charAt(i % len) - 'a');\n            }\n    